      "id": 1,
      "text": "Benzonia began in 1858 as a Christian colony modeled after Oberlin, Ohio, aiming to blend education with community life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzonia%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1891, the village renamed its school Benzonia College, reflecting its growing educational ambitions.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzonia%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1900, Benzonia College became Benzonia Academy, a preparatory school preparing students for college.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzonia%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historian Bruce Catton wrote \"Waiting for the Morning Train,\" detailing his childhood in Benzonia while his father taught at the academy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzonia%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Champion Hill Civil War Cemetery in Benzonia holds the graves of several Civil War veterans, offering a quiet tribute.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://michiganinsider.blog/things-to-do-benzonia-michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
